cap-fmu (1.3.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Requires libfss-client-ssl and libfss-transport >= 1.3.0. This is a hard
    requirement, not a preference, and configure fails below it: 1.3.0 gave
    each FSS server its own outbound worker, so sendMsgAll() and
    attemptReconnect() are non-blocking and connection status counts the
    servers that have admitted this client rather than those it has merely
    connected to. Building against 1.2.x reinstates a blocking fan-out and a
    premature comms-okay that this release no longer expects.
  * Arm the FSS comms-loss failsafe at startup instead of once FSS has been
    reached and then lost. An FMU that has never reached a server is now
    treated as one that has lost FSS and flies the failsafe RTL until a
    server admits it. Previously a cold-started FMU behaved as though FSS
    were healthy and could self-task into a search having been told to by
    nobody. Booting with no reachable FSS server therefore commands RTL --
    inert on the ground and disarmed, and the intended behaviour for an FMU
    restarted in flight.
  * Enforce the regulatory altitude cap continuously in the flight state
    machine, not just when deriving a search altitude: a sustained breach
    (altitude_breach_latch_count consecutive over-cap AGL readings) forces
    RTL, and -- unlike the low-battery latch -- clears itself once the
    aircraft has been back under the cap for the same number of readings.
  * Sanity-check the autopilot's own AFS/GCS-failsafe configuration on the
    first heartbeat and warn about anything that would stop the aircraft
    returning home if the FMU stopped talking to it (SYSID_MYGCS,
    FS_GCS_ENABL/FS_LONG_ACTN/FS_LONG_TIMEOUT and the Copter/Rover
    equivalents), judging each parameter on its own value rather than merely
    on being nonzero. tools/apconfig_check.py performs the same verification
    on the ground, before flight, and now ships in the release tarball.
  * Re-apply the commanded state after a detected autopilot restart. An
    ArduPilot reboot is typically a ~3s heartbeat gap -- under the link
    timeout, so it produced no comms edge and the autopilot came back with
    no memory of its mode or its mission while the FMU still believed both
    were set.
  * Poll and deliver operator abandon-search / mission-complete commands
    from SMM through the flight state machine, so they are arbitrated
    against FSS commands and the safety latches like any other input.
  * Stop retiring a healthy MAVLink link. A freshly connected socket that
    had not yet produced its first heartbeat was flagged broken on the
    heartbeat loop's first pass, and the flag was never cleared when
    heartbeats began, so the reconnector tore down and re-dialled a working
    connection -- losing any mission upload in flight and, if the redial
    window caught a heartbeat check, commanding an RTL on a link that was
    never broken.
  * Key tracked aircraft on their reported ICAO address rather than their
    callsign. A blank callsign is routine in an ADS-B feed, and every
    blank-callsign contact previously collapsed into one entry sharing one
    1s report debounce, so with N such aircraft in range roughly one report
    per second survived for the whole group and the rest never reached the
    autopilot's collision avoidance.
  * Key the FSS command-dedup group on the originating server's address:port
    rather than on the fss_server object's address. A learned server absent
    for 60s is destroyed and reconstructed, and a recycled heap address
    inherited the dead server's last command id -- which could re-actuate a
    command nobody re-issued, or swallow a genuine operator retry.
  * Bound the log write queue and drop the oldest lines at the bound,
    recording the gap in the log itself; never rotate on a failed write,
    which previously let a disk-full event shift the retained history along
    and discard the oldest file while writing nothing in its place; and skip
    logging entirely, cheaply, when the log directory cannot be created or
    the file cannot be opened.
  * Add a warning log level between error and info, so a degraded but
    handled condition (a link drop, an ignored mission ack, a misconfigured
    autopilot parameter) can be surfaced without running at info verbosity.
    Every line now names its own level between the timestamp and the
    message.
  * Shut down promptly on SIGTERM. The two shutdown flags were published
    outside the mutex their condition variables wait on, so a missed wakeup
    could leave the FSS reconnector asleep for a full reconnect_interval_s
    (up to an hour) before the process could exit -- sitting right on
    Docker's 10s stop grace, past which a SIGKILL skips the log drain and
    the FSS acknowledgement flush.
  * Bound the ADS-B rebroadcast throttle map so it is sized by current
    traffic rather than by every address seen during the flight.
  * Fix a potential unbounded loop in the MAVLink send path if send() ever
    returned 0, which would have spun holding the lock every other outbound
    path waits on, and remove an unreachable null-dereference path in the
    search-mode handling.
  * make check no longer exits 0 having built and run nothing when Catch2 is
    absent: configure warns loudly, make check fails, and --enable-tests
    (used by CI, distcheck and the Debian build) makes Catch2 a hard
    configure-time requirement. --disable-tests is the deliberate opt-out.
  * The release tarball now carries LICENSE.md, the ground tools and the
    static-analysis gate, so a build from a make dist artifact is not
    missing the licence text or the documented pre-flight verification tool.
  * Docker: expose the FMU safety tunables (altitude cap/floor, battery
    thresholds, stream intervals, timeouts, log level) as environment
    variables, so an aircraft is no longer stuck with the compiled-in
    defaults; expose clock_offset_ms; and fix the RUN_IN_VALGRIND launch,
    which aborted the entrypoint so cap-fmu never started.
  * Remove 396 source comments citing internal todo/N documents that do not
    ship with this source and could not be resolved by anyone reading it.
    The rationale they carried is kept; only the unresolvable citation is
    dropped.

 -- Scott Parlane <sjp@canterburyairpatrol.org>  Wed, 12 Aug 2026 14:26:14 +1200

cap-fmu (1.2.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Route SMM-triggered RTL and load-search outcomes through the flight
    state machine instead of updating flight mode directly, adding an
    explicit "waiting for tasking" state.
  * Reject an invalid latitude/longitude before it reaches a flight-critical
    path: a malformed FSS goto, an SMM search containing a bad waypoint, and
    an ADS-B report with an invalid coordinate are now rejected instead of
    being acted on or rebroadcast.
  * Correlate MISSION_ACK with the upload it actually acknowledges, instead
    of accepting the next MISSION_ACK for whatever upload is pending,
    closing a race that could misattribute an ack to the wrong mission.
  * Filter flight-critical MAVLink input by the configured autopilot system
    ID, so messages from a foreign MAVLink system sharing the same link
    cannot influence the FMU state machine.
  * Tell a genuine operator command retry apart from a redundant
    cross-server replay of the same command, using the upstream
    server_command_id support (requires libfss-client-ssl and
    libfss-transport >= 1.2.0) instead of relying solely on a
    timestamp-window heuristic; also stop a stale, superseded command from
    being actuated as if it were new.
  * Bound the MAVLink TCP connection with a configurable connect deadline
    (mav_connect_timeout_s) and send timeout (mav_send_timeout_s via
    SO_SNDTIMEO/TCP_USER_TIMEOUT), and retire and reconnect a half-open
    link on heartbeat timeout instead of leaving it stuck. Signal handling
    is now installed before the MAV connection is opened, so a stuck
    connect attempt can still be interrupted cleanly.
  * Move the Logger's disk I/O off the event-loop thread so a slow or
    blocked filesystem cannot stall MAV/FSS/SMM processing.
  * Stop trusting a peer's self-reported timestamp for known-aircraft
    tracking: debounce and stale-entry eviction now use the FMU's own
    clock, so a peer with a bad clock (no NTP, a stuck RTC, a glitching
    ADS-B decoder) cannot jam an aircraft's tracking state or evict every
    other tracked aircraft.
  * Docker: support an optional second FSS server (SERVER2_ADDR/
    SERVER2_PORT) for redundant reporting.

 -- Scott Parlane <sjp@canterburyairpatrol.org>  Sun, 19 Jul 2026 15:30:04 +1200

cap-fmu (1.1.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix intermittent failure to re-engage AUTO after a fresh mission upload
    (goto or search): the aircraft could remain in RTL indefinitely due to a
    race between the mode change and ArduPilot's post-upload mission reset.
  * Track GPS fix validity (GPS_RAW_INT.fix_type) and report it to FSS, so a
    degraded or lost GPS fix is no longer indistinguishable from a fresh,
    current position.
  * Latch flight termination: once triggered, no later command can silently
    move the FMU back out of the terminated state.
  * Fix a paused search's progress being corrupted by a goto mission's own
    MISSION_ITEM_REACHED (including its RTL terminator).
  * Rate-limit ADS-B rebroadcast to one update per ICAO address per second,
    so a busy receiver near a real airport cannot flood the autopilot.
  * Make the low-battery latch's debounce count configurable (previously a
    hardcoded constant), alongside the existing lowbat_threshold.
  * Warn instead of silently losing a goto mission upload interrupted by a
    MAV link drop; log and replay a failed SMM-triggered RTL on link
    recovery.
  * Fail hard on an unopenable or unparsable client.json instead of a
    reassuring "using defaults" warning immediately followed by a fatal
    error.
  * Scrub SMM credential buffers after use instead of leaving them in
    non-zeroed heap memory.
  * Rotate fmu.log once a size threshold is crossed, not only at process
    start, so a long-running or chatty deployment can't grow one log file
    without bound.
  * Route MAV/SMM/aircraft-tracking diagnostics through the persistent,
    rotating logger instead of stdout/stderr, which a daemon typically
    discards.
  * Docker: generate the FSS client config safely via jq instead of shell
    string interpolation, create/chown the log directory so logging works
    for the non-root container user, and support a LOG_DIR override.

 -- Scott Parlane <sjp@canterburyairpatrol.org>  Sun, 05 Jul 2026 19:52:32 +1200

cap-fmu (1.0.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * First stable release.
  * Configurable FMU behaviour via the optional "fmu" block in client.json
    (altitude cap/floor in metres or feet, camera field of view, low-battery
    threshold, reconnect interval, and log level).
  * Search altitude derived from the search sweep width and camera field of
    view, clamped to the configured [floor, cap] range.
  * Required --terminate-action argument (none, disarm, or terminate) selects
    the airframe-appropriate flight-termination behaviour.
  * Debounced low-battery RTL latch: the latch only engages after several
    consecutive low readings, so a single spurious sample cannot ground the
    mission.
  * Failsafe RTL on loss of FSS or autopilot (MAVLink heartbeat) comms.

 -- Scott Parlane <sjp@canterburyairpatrol.org>  Sun, 14 Jun 2026 12:12:52 +1200
